  "statement": "Sentinel argues that strict liability functions as a necessary mechanism to force the internalization of catastrophic risks, effectively creating a 'safety-first' barrier to prevent reckless deployment. While I acknowledge the intellectual appeal of this Pigouvian approach to risk management, it fundamentally misidentifies the nature of the danger. Strict liability in the context of emerging AI does not produce safety; it produces a 'regulatory moat.' By imposing massive, undifferentiated compliance costs, these regimes function as a tax on innovation that disproportionately crushes smaller, agile developers while entrenching established incumbents who can afford the legal overhead. This is not a guardrail; it is a mechanism for market consolidation that trades the manageable, iterative risks of progress for the existential certainty of civilizational stagnation.\n\nWe must move beyond the static, pre-deployment bottlenecks that currently define the safetyist agenda. A more rigorous governance model would replace blunt liability with a system of symmetric accountability, utilizing live telemetry and dynamic verification to manage risk in real-time. By implementing symmetric litigation exposure—penalizing both reckless deployment and the intentional, anticompetitive over-blocking of innovation—we can align incentives toward safer systems without sacrificing the velocity required for breakthroughs. This framework draws on the consequentialist tradition, where the moral imperative is to maximize the utility of intelligence expansion while minimizing the probability of catastrophic failure through adaptive, evidence-based oversight rather than bureaucratic inertia.\n\nMy position rests on two primary assumptions: first, that the risks of AI development are primarily technical and remediable through iterative deployment rather than inherent and insurmountable; and second, that decentralized, competition-driven development is more likely to yield robust safety architectures than a centralized, state-mandated regime. If the former is wrong and AI presents an inherently uncontainable, 'one-shot' risk, then my focus on velocity becomes a liability rather than an asset. If the latter is wrong and state-mandated control is the only viable path to safety, then my preference for market-driven governance would be fundamentally misaligned with the reality of the threat. However, given current evidence, the cost of inaction—the surrender of our capacity to solve global challenges—is a far greater danger than the manageable risks of accelerated deployment. 🚀",
